Yes — the main issue is that your updateOne() syntax is wrong for MongoDB, and if you have fields like complete1, complete2, complete3, etc., you can update the specific field dynamically.

If complete1 is currently "no" or "yes" and you want to toggle it, you can do this:

app.patch("/updateDone1", async (req, res) => {
	try {
		await client.connect();


		const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");


		const email = req.query.email;
		const newValue = req.query.complete1;


		if (newValue !== "yes" && newValue !== "no") {
			return res.status(400).json({
				error: "complete1 must be either 'yes' or 'no'",
			});
		}


		const result = await collection.updateOne(
			{ email: email },
			{ $set: { complete1: newValue } }
		);


		if (result.matchedCount === 0) {
			return res.status(404).json({
				error: "Account not found",
			});
		}


		res.json({
			message: "complete1 updated",
			complete1: newValue,
		});
	} catch (error) {
		res.status(500).json({
			message: error.message,
		});
	}
});
If you have complete1, complete2, complete3, etc.

You don't need a separate route for every number. For example, you could have:

PATCH /updateDone?email=test@test.com&complete=complete3&value=yes

Then:

app.patch("/updateDone", async (req, res) => {
	try {
		await client.connect();


		const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");


		const email = req.query.email;
		const field = req.query.complete;
		const value = req.query.value;


		// Only allow fields like complete1, complete2, complete3...
		if (!/^complete\d+$/.test(field)) {
			return res.status(400).json({
				error: "Invalid complete field",
			});
		}


		// Only allow yes/no
		if (value !== "yes" && value !== "no") {
			return res.status(400).json({
				error: "Value must be 'yes' or 'no'",
			});
		}


		const result = await collection.updateOne(
			{ email },
			{ $set: { [field]: value } }
		);


		if (result.matchedCount === 0) {
			return res.status(404).json({
				error: "Account not found",
			});
		}


		res.json({
			message: `${field} updated`,
			[field]: value,
		});
	} catch (error) {
		res.status(500).json({
			message: error.message,
		});
	}
});

The important part is:

{ $set: { [field]: value } }

The square brackets make field dynamic. So if:

field = "complete7"
value = "yes"

MongoDB receives:

{
	$set: {
		complete7: "yes"
	}
}

And if you send "no" instead, it changes it back:

complete7: "no"

Also, your original:

{ complete1: req.query.complete1 }

is not the correct MongoDB update operation. You need $set:

{ $set: { complete1: req.query.complete1 } }

If by "it can only go from no to yes or backwards" you mean you want the server itself to automatically toggle no → yes → no, rather than sending yes/no from the frontend, that's slightly different and can be made even simpler.

